00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
Let me tell you a story. When Vladimir Smirnov fled Russia during the revolution,

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.960
he didn't exactly pack a suitcase full of valuables. He left with very little, but in his pocket,

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.920
he carried something very special - a recipe. A recipe for something almost completely unknown

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:19.680
in the west at that time - vodka. Now, this wasn't just any family hobby. Back home,

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:25.040
the Smirnovs had been distilling vodka since 1864. The House of Smirnov supplied the imperial

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:31.040
court of the Tsars. Think prestige, wealth, and some serious credibility. But the Bolsheviks

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:36.480
weren't handing out sympathy cards. The revolution swept everything away - his business, his home,

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.400
his entire country. So Vladimir started again, first in Poland, then in France, and eventually,

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.600
through a string of licensing deals - and let's be honest, a bit of good luck - his vodka landed

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:54.880
in the United States. Now, here's a snag though. America didn't want vodka. This was the 1930s.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:01.520
Whisky was king. Rum had pirates. Gin had speakeasies. But vodka, nobody had ever heard of it.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:07.280
It had no image, no story, no market. Honestly, trying to sell vodka in the US back then was

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:12.160
like trying to sell a fax machine to Gen Z today. People would just look at you blankly.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:16.240
So what did Vladimir and his partners do? They didn't try to force vodka down people's throats,

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:21.280
literally or figuratively. Instead, they rebranded it. They called it - and here's the genius - white

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:28.240
whisky. And the pitch, no taste, no smell, that was a hook. Something familiar - whisky, and something

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:33.920
just different enough - white. Absolutely genius. Half a step forward, but not so far that people

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:39.520
rejected it, but far enough to get them curious. Then along the way came the Moscow mule. A drink

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:45.440
built entirely to sell vodka. Copper mug, fresh lime, ginger beer, absolutely delicious, and more

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:50.720
importantly, very cool. By the time the mule took off, people were finally ready to hear the word

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:55.680
vodka. And the rest, as they say, is history. Today, Smirnoff is the best-selling vodka in the

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:00.880
world, over 28 million cases a year. All of it traced back to one man who understood something

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:06.800
crucial - meet people where they are, and then walk them half a step forward. And that's an idea

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:12.080
worth holding on to, just half a step. Your clients don't live in your world. They're not reading your

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:16.240
strategy deck. They don't know your jargon. They're not walking around all day thinking about your

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:22.960
work. So when you put an idea forward, don't start in your world, start in theirs. White whisky

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:29.040
wasn't vodka's identity, but it was an invitation, the bridge, if you like. Nobody was ready for

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:34.800
vodka, but they were ready for something that sounded like whisky, just lighter, cleaner, fresher.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:40.640
White whisky. Half a step forward, then came the next step, the Moscow mule, then eventually

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:47.040
global dominance. Smirnoff didn't push, it guided. Now that's exactly the same in consulting.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:52.560
Your insight, your model, your big recommendation, that is your vodka. It may well be brilliant,

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:56.960
but the client does not live in your world. They're not reading your deck, and they do not share your

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:02.880
language. So the craft is not to push a finished idea at them, it's to meet them where they actually

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:09.120
are, and walk them half a step forward, and then another. Do that, and the idea stops feeling like

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.120
something being done to them, and starts feeling like something they arrived at themselves.

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:18.480
In the client's world, that is the whole difference between being lectured and being led.
